  "metadata": {
    "title": "Annular Solar Eclipse 2023: recordings of the entire medium-wave AM broadcast band from near Sacramento, CA, USA",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.14171587",
    "publication_date": "2024-11-15",
    "description": "<p>files recorded by Richard Cook, KE6EE</p>\n<p>Grid square of location recorded:&nbsp; CM98jp</p>\n<p>Maximum obscuration of the sun was 80% at 1620UT.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>\n<p>These data files are recordings of the entire medium wave broadcast band (535-1705kHz) made during the annular solar eclipse of 14 October 2024 from 1440UT to 1720UT from near Sacramento, CA, USA.&nbsp; They are part of a set of such recordings made from 13 Oct 0400UT to 15 Oct 1900UT.</p>\n<p>Data Quality:&nbsp;&nbsp;There were data dropouts at Oct 13 16:22-23UT, Oct 14 16:28UT, and Oct 14 16:45-16:48UT, plus a few shorter ones. There were many weak spurious carriers.</p>\n<p>Data files are .wav format, but the archived files are .zip, with about five .wav files packaged together in each .zip file, using the prefix \"KE6EE-wideband_500_1750kHz&ldquo;.&nbsp; The recordings not included here are available to provide reference data for the eclipse recordings. &nbsp;Please contact HamSCI at am-eclipse@hamsci.org if you require this data.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Antenna: &nbsp;W6LVP loop, orientated East/West and six feet above ground.</p>\n<p>Receiver:&nbsp; Sdrplay duo &nbsp;&nbsp; RF gain = 0.0dB, with center frequency 1125kHz, recording a 1250kHz wide passband, using SDRuno software, V1.42 1128.&nbsp; See included file \"IMG_20231014_094558810.jpg\" for further settings.&nbsp; The receiver was frequency locked to a Bodnar MiniGPS Reference Clock.&nbsp; &nbsp;Computer time accuracy was determined by Windows NTP using time.nist.gov .</p>",
